<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Democratic Republic of Yemen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1994">1994</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Democratic Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> was a breakaway state proclaimed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> on 21 May 1994 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-salim-al-beidh">Ali Salim al-Beidh</a>, the former leader of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-yemen">South Yemen</a>, during the civil war that followed unification. It controlled much of the former south but won no international recognition, and it collapsed when northern forces captured Aden on 7 July 1994.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-yemen.jpg" alt="Democratic Republic of Yemen" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: National Front - الجبهة القومية · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_South_Yemen.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Al-Beidh announced the state on Aden Radio, proclaiming the creation of the Democratic Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> as an independent state with its capital at Aden. It was meant as an alternative regime for the whole country rather than only a southern state: al-Beidh called it the nucleus for a unified Yemen, built on the Document of Pledge and Accord, which he described as a document of national consensus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Al-Beidh was head of state and led a Presidential Council whose members were Abdal-Qawi Makkawi, a former leader of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/front-for-the-liberation-of-occupied-south-yemen">FLOSY</a>, Abdurrahman Al-Jifri, Sulaiman Nasir Muhammad and Salim Salih Muhammad. A government was announced on 2 June 1994, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haidar-abu-bakr-al-attas">Haidar Abu Bakr al-Attas</a> as Prime Minister and Minister of Finance. Besides figures of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemeni-socialist-party">Yemeni Socialist Party</a> such as al-Beidh and al-Attas, the leadership drew in members of other South Yemeni and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-arabia">South Arabian</a> groups, among them Abdallah al-Asnaj, who had strongly opposed the Socialist Party's one-party rule in the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-yemen">People's Democratic Republic of Yemen</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The war of the summer of 1994 between the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> and the new state lasted from May until 7 July and cost between 1,000 and 7,000 lives. Supporters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-nasir-muhammad">Ali Nasir Muhammad</a>'s faction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemeni-socialist-party">Yemeni Socialist Party</a> fought with the government's forces and tribes, and Islamist parties were hostile to the southern government.</p><p>The government of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, which won the war, describes it as a war in defence of unity. Many people from the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">governorates</a> call it a war of occupation and claim that lands and properties were seized for officials and tribal sheikhs from the north. After the war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-abdullah-saleh">Ali Abdullah Saleh</a> appointed prime ministers from the south in an effort to win southern support.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Democratic Republic of Yemen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 21 May al-Beidh proclaimed a breakaway <b>Democratic Republic of Yemen</b> in Aden, but it won no international recognition, and northern forces, joined by tribal levies and Islamist volunteers, captured Aden on 7 July 1994.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-yemen">Republic of Yemen · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The constitution of 1970 renamed the country the People’s <b>Democratic Republic of Yemen</b> and created a Supreme People’s Council as its legislature.</blockquote><a href="/empire/south-yemen">South Yemen · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
